    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon.jpg" alt="Napoleon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacques-Louis_David_-_The_Emperor_Napoleon_in_His_Study_at_the_Tuileries_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jacques-Louis David, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Grand Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> grew out of the Landgraviate of Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a>, one of the branches into which the Landgraviate of Hesse had been divided in 1567. Landgrave Louis X, who succeeded in 1790, lost his small territories west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> to revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichsdeputationshauptschluss">Imperial Recess of 1803</a> he was compensated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-westphalia">Duchy of Westphalia</a>, formerly ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/electorate-of-cologne">Archbishop-Electors of Cologne</a>, together with lands of the Archbishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> and the Electoral Palatinate on the right bank. In 1806 he joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a> and took the title Grand Duke Louis I, gaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> and the lands of neighbouring counts and princes who were mediatised, among them Erbach, parts of Isenburg and Solms, the Burgraviate of Friedberg and Hesse-Homburg. Like other south and west German princes he changed sides in November 1813 and kept his title. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> redrew his borders: in 1816 he ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and Hesse-Homburg was restored as a separate landgraviate, while he received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhenish-hesse">Rhenish Hesse</a> on the left bank of the Rhine, with Mainz, Worms, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bingen-am-rhein">Bingen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alzey">Alzey</a>. The state, officially named the Grand Duchy of Hesse and by Rhine, was thus composed of three provinces: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/starkenburg">Starkenburg</a> around Darmstadt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-hesse">Upper Hesse</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giessen">Giessen</a>, separated from the rest by a strip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> and Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a> territory, and Rhenish Hesse.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/main-neckar-railway"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/main-neckar-railway.jpg" alt="Main-Neckar Railway" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/main-neckar-railway">Main-Neckar Railway</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bahntrasse.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Brandrodungswanderfeldhackbau, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The grand duchy was a small state of about 7,700 square kilometres, whose population grew to about 1.3 million by 1910. Its three provinces differed sharply. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/starkenburg">Starkenburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-hesse">Upper Hesse</a> were mainly Lutheran, with poor upland farming in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vogelsberg">Vogelsberg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odenwald">Odenwald</a> that drove many families to emigrate to America in the nineteenth century, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhenish-hesse">Rhenish Hesse</a> was largely Catholic, prosperous and devoted to grain and wine; it became one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s largest wine-growing regions. Rhenish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> also kept the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a> introduced under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> until the German Civil Code took effect in 1900, so the state had two legal systems. Hesse concluded a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/customs-union">customs union</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1828, one of the steps towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zollverein">German Customs Union</a> of 1834. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/main-neckar-railway">Main-Neckar Railway</a> linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> opened in 1846, and the private <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hessian-ludwig-railway">Hessian Ludwig Railway</a> connected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>. Industry grew in the towns along the Main and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. The pharmacy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emanuel-merck">Emanuel Merck</a> in Darmstadt began industrial production of alkaloids in the 1820s and became a major chemical and pharmaceutical firm, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-opel">Adam Opel</a> started making sewing machines at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russelsheim">Rüsselsheim</a> in 1862 and motor cars in 1899, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offenbach-am-main">Offenbach</a> became known for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leather">leather goods</a>. Mainz, a fortress and river port, remained the largest city, while Darmstadt grew as a residential and administrative capital.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-laufach"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-laufach.jpg" alt="Battle of Laufach" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-laufach">Battle of Laufach</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frohnhofen,_Angriff_hessisch-darmst%C3%A4dter_Truppen_auf_die_Preu%C3%9Fen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Otto Fikentscher, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The grand duchy's military role was that of a small allied contingent rather than an independent power. As a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> supplied troops to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, which fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1809 and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and a Hessian contingent marched into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1812, where it suffered heavy losses. After changing sides in November 1813, Hessian troops served with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> in the final campaigns against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Under the German Confederation, the Hessian division formed part of a mixed federal army corps, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> became a federal fortress garrisoned jointly by Austrian and Prussian troops, so that the strongest fortification on Hessian soil was not under Hessian command. In the war of 1866 Hesse joined Austria, and its division, part of the federal Eighth Army Corps, was beaten by Prussian forces in the Main campaign, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-laufach">Laufach</a> and Frohnhofen in July. By a military convention with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1867 the Hessian troops were brought into the Prussian system, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> the Hessian division fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gravelotte">Battle of Gravelotte–St. Privat</a> in August 1870. After 1871 it formed the 25th Division of the imperial army under Prussian command, keeping its Hessian identity and recruitment, and Hessian regiments served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/der-hessische-landbote"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/der-hessische-landbote.jpg" alt="Der Hessische Landbote" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/der-hessische-landbote">Der Hessische Landbote</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Landbote.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Georg Büchner, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Grand Duke Louis I governed through a bureaucracy that integrated his varied territories, abolished many noble and corporate privileges and introduced uniform administration. Under pressure from popular unrest and from the federal constitution, which required representative assemblies, he issued a constitution on 17 December 1820, after a first, more restrictive draft had been rejected earlier that year. It created a parliament of two chambers, an upper chamber of princes, nobles and appointed members and an elected lower chamber, with rights over taxation and legislation. Under Louis II, from 1830, the conservative minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-du-thil">Karl du Thil</a> dominated government, and the authorities suppressed radical opposition, most famously the revolutionary pamphlet <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/der-hessische-landbote">Der Hessische Landbote</a></em> of 1834 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georg-buchner">Georg Büchner</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-ludwig-weidig">Friedrich Ludwig Weidig</a>. In March 1848 the liberal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-von-gagern">Heinrich von Gagern</a> became chief minister before he was elected president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt-parliament">Frankfurt National Assembly</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-iii-grand-duke-of-hesse">Louis III</a> succeeded the same year. After the revolution <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reinhard-von-dalwigk">Reinhard von Dalwigk</a>, chief minister from 1850 to 1871, restored conservative rule and followed an anti-Prussian policy. Within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> the grand duchy kept its own government and parliament. The last grand dukes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-iv-holy-roman-emperor">Louis IV</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-louis-grand-duke-of-hesse">Ernest Louis</a>, were constitutional monarchs who left much of the business of government to their ministers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dantons-death"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dantons-death.jpg" alt="Danton's Death" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dantons-death">Danton's Death</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-Z0420-027,_Berlin,_%22Dantons_Tod%22.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Katja Rehfeld, CC BY-SA 3.0 de</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The grand duchy's wider influence came through science, art and dynastic marriage. At the state <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-giessen">University of Giessen</a> the chemist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justus-von-liebig">Justus von Liebig</a>, professor from 1824 to 1852, built a teaching laboratory that trained students from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and America and helped establish modern chemical education and agricultural chemistry. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/technical-university-of-darmstadt">Technical University of Darmstadt</a> created a chair of electrical engineering in 1882, often described as the first in the world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georg-buchner">Georg Büchner</a>, born near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a>, wrote <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dantons-death">Danton's Death</a></em> and <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woyzeck">Woyzeck</a></em> before his early death in exile in 1837. Grand Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-louis-grand-duke-of-hesse">Ernest Louis</a> made Darmstadt a centre of modern design by founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt-artists-colony">Darmstadt Artists' Colony</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathildenhohe">Mathildenhöhe</a> in 1899; its exhibition of 1901, with buildings by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-maria-olbrich">Joseph Maria Olbrich</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-behrens">Peter Behrens</a>, was a landmark of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/art-nouveau">Jugendstil</a> and early modern architecture, crowned in 1908 by the Wedding Tower. The ruling family married into the great royal houses. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-iv-holy-roman-emperor">Louis IV</a> married Princess Alice, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>, and their daughters included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandra-feodorovna-alix-of-hesse">Alix</a>, who became Empress Alexandra of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1894, and Elisabeth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchess-elizabeth-feodorovna">Grand Duchess Elizabeth Feodorovna</a>. A morganatic branch, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battenberg-family">Battenbergs</a>, produced Alexander, the first Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-louis-of-battenberg">Louis of Battenberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sea-lord">First Sea Lord</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>, whose descendants took the name Mountbatten.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-world-war.jpg" alt="First World War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bataille_de_Verdun_1916.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Collection DocAnciens/docpix.fr, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The grand duchy's independence ended with the Prussian victory of 1866. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> had joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and lost: by the peace treaty of September 1866 it ceded Hesse-Homburg, which it had inherited only months earlier, and some districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-hesse">Upper Hesse</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, and the province of Upper Hesse north of the Main had to join the North German Confederation, while the rest of the state remained outside. Hesse was thus divided between two political systems until 1871. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reinhard-von-dalwigk">Dalwigk</a> hoped for French support against Prussia, but in 1870 the grand duchy fought on the German side and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> with the other southern states. Within the empire it was a member state of modest weight, with its own dynasty, parliament and administration. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> brought shortages, heavy losses and a painful conflict of loyalties for a family closely tied to both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. When revolution reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a> in November 1918, the workers' and soldiers' council declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-louis-grand-duke-of-hesse">Ernest Louis</a> deposed on 9 November; he never formally abdicated and continued to live in Hesse. The grand duchy became the People's State of Hesse, a republic within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Germany</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhenish-hesse">Rhenish Hesse</a> was assigned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/starkenburg">Starkenburg</a> and Upper Hesse became part of the modern state of Hesse.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Grand Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> turned Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a> into a sovereign constitutional state on both banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, joining Lutheran uplands and Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhenish-hesse">Rhenish Hesse</a>, and gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> Liebig's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giessen">Giessen</a> laboratory, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt-artists-colony">Darmstadt Artists' Colony</a> and dynastic links that reached the thrones of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</em></p>

